  "answer_text": "What you're seeing is most likely **limescale**, also known as **mineral deposits** \u2014 and it's completely harmless! Here's a breakdown:\n\n### What It Is\n\nThe white residue you see on glassware is the result of minerals carried through your water, typically called **limescale**. The primary culprit is **calcium carbonate (CaCO\u2083)**, a naturally occurring mineral that is a key component of **hard water** \u2014 water that contains high concentrations of minerals like calcium and magnesium.\n\nEven filtered water from your fridge still contains these dissolved minerals. Most refrigerator filters are designed to remove things like chlorine, odors, and certain contaminants, but they don't fully remove hardness minerals.\n\n### Why It Builds Up Over Time\n\nMost municipal and well water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions. When water hardness reaches moderate to high levels, these minerals remain dissolved only as long as the water stays cool and stable. As the water sits in your glass over days, it slowly evaporates and the minerals are left behind, gradually building up into that visible white film. The result is the formation of calcium carbonate and related mineral deposits, which adhere to glass surfaces. Once these minerals have precipitated, they are no longer removable by simply rinsing, because they are no longer dissolved in water.\n\n### Is It Dangerous?\n\nNot at all. Higher amounts of mineral deposits usually signify higher levels of water hardness, but limescale is harmless to human health.\n\n### How to Clean It\n\nYou can make a simple cleaning solution by combining **equal parts vinegar and water**. Submerge the affected glassware in the solution and let it soak overnight for best results. After soaking, rinse with warm water until the limescale is gone. The mild acid in the vinegar dissolves the mineral deposits effectively.\n\n### A Quick DIY Test\n\nIf you're ever unsure whether what you're seeing is truly limescale, you can place a sample of the residue in a cup of vinegar \u2014 if the particles are from hard water, they will bubble up and dissolve into the vinegar.\n\nIn short, it's just the natural minerals from your water supply doing what minerals do \u2014 nothing to worry about!",
